## 2. Core Modules

### 2.1. Meal Planner & Grocery Sync
The design deliberately keeps ingredients as **free text** and uses an **LLM with a human in the loop** as the aggregation engine, rather than encoding a canonical ingredient catalog, unit-conversion tables, or densities. *Design note: do not "upgrade" this into a controlled-vocabulary ingredient engine — the human-in-the-loop review is the intended smoothing mechanism for fuzzy matching and unit math.*

* **Recipe Store:** Lightweight recipe records — title, **free-text ingredient lines** (no parsing into structured quantities), servings, tags/allergens, rating, and source. America's Test Kitchen is treated as a **manual-entry source** (paywalled and bot-hostile); ingestion is manual entry or paste-and-parse, with schema.org/Recipe JSON-LD URL import as a possible nice-to-have.
* **Scheduler:** A calendar interface to assign recipes to days. Planning also runs *backwards* from on-hand items (see Use-It-Up inventory in §2.2).
* **LLM Aggregation Worker (human-in-the-loop):** A backend worker calls the **Claude API** with the week's planned recipes (plus any use-it-up items) and returns a draft, aggregated shopping list. The human reviews/corrects it before it becomes the shopping list.

### 2.2. Kitchen Inventory & Shopping Board
Three small, single-purpose lists — note the first two are *opposite* in intent:

* **Staples (passive — buy-suppression):** A curated set tracked as binary `have / running-low` (Tier 1, no quantities). Pre-checks the items you usually have so the weekly list doesn't re-ask every week. Purpose: push items *off* the list.
* **Use-It-Up inventory (active — planning input):** Notable on-hand perishables/freezer items you want to cook down (e.g., flank steak, chicken thighs), with optional location and date-acquired. **Feeds the planner as an input** — Claude builds the week around using these first. An item leaves the list when assigned to a meal. Purpose: pull the *plan* toward itself. *(Optional later: "been in the freezer a while" nudges via the ambient feed.)*
* **Unified Shopping Board (kanban):** One standing board with columns **Need → This week → Bought**. Both ad-hoc run-outs ("out of dish soap" — ideal e-ink quick-capture) and the weekly Claude-generated items flow onto it, tagged by source. The **This week** column is what Saturday's shop / the Whole Foods order draws from; checking items off is the same grocery check-off described in §6.2.

### 2.3. Whole Foods Actuation Layer
* **Cart Automation:** A background task (headless browser automation via Playwright) that logs into Whole Foods, translates the **This week** column of the shopping board (§2.2) into real products, matches them against user preferences, and populates the delivery cart for **manual final checkout** — the human verifies the order before purchase. (Reliability/auth specifics are an open spike; see §8.)

### 2.4. Personal Finance (Actual Budget Integration)
The strategy is **wrap and delegate**: Actual owns the budgeting UX and remains the source of truth and the place you edit; Household OS wraps it so it feels like one product and mirrors summaries to the top level. Consistent with §2.6 — read-only mirror, edits delegated to Actual, never written back.

* **Local Ledger:** A self-hosted instance of **Actual Budget** in Docker (`actualbudget/actual-server`, port 5006, data on a persistent volume at `/data`), hosted at a dedicated subdomain (e.g., `budget.<household-domain>`) behind the same reverse proxy and Cloudflare Access SSO as the rest of Household OS. Bank linking (SimpleFIN/GoCardless) is configured **natively inside Actual** — not reimplemented here. *Note: Actual has its own login (a single shared server password) layered behind Cloudflare Access; multi-user/OpenID is unnecessary given §4.2's no-roles model.*
* **"Wrapped," not embedded:** Actual requires a secure context plus `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` and `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp` headers to enable SharedArrayBuffer (its WASM SQLite engine). Those headers **preclude iframe-embedding** Actual inside the Household OS shell, and the reverse proxy must pass them through **without duplication** (duplicate COOP/COEP headers trigger a `SharedArrayBufferMissing` fatal error). "Feels in-product" therefore means a **shared subdomain, matching nav styling, and deep-links** — not an embedded frame.
* **No REST API — a synced local copy.** Actual exposes **no REST/HTTP API**. The server is a sync store that cannot compute budgets; all budget logic lives in the **`@actual-app/api` Node.js library**, which downloads a *full local copy* of the budget into a `dataDir`, syncs, then answers queries like `getBudgetMonth('YYYY-MM')`. The finance worker therefore maintains its own synced copy on the volume and calls `sync()` before each read. *(Because the official API is Node-only, this nudges the Hub backend toward Node — see §3.1. A Python Hub would need `actualpy` or an `actual-http-api` sidecar.)*
* **Summary Widgets:** The top-level dashboard surfaces category balances and monthly targets (read-only, via the synced `@actual-app/api` copy above), each **deep-linking into the wrapped Actual app** for detail and editing.
* **Overspend Alerts:** A worker syncs the local copy and checks category balances against their monthly targets, posting overspend / near-limit alerts into the ambient Family Feed (§2.5). Read-only — it observes Actual, never writes to it. It reaches Actual over the **internal container network**, not through Cloudflare Access (which would block a non-browser client).

### 2.5. Unified Calendar, Tasks, & Home Status
* **Aggregator:** Flattens data from each external surface into a unified "Family Feed," following the **mirror-or-own** rule in §2.6 — Calendar is mirrored read-only, Tasks/chores and the shopping board are owned.
* **Home Assistant Bridge:** Pulls critical ambient notifications from Home Assistant (e.g., utility tracking, pet feeding status, security/door states).

### 2.6. Integration & Sync Model
**Hard rule: no two-way sync, ever.** Bidirectional sync is where all conflict-resolution complexity lives. Every external surface sits in exactly one of two buckets:

* **Mirror (read-only):** The external product remains source of truth (Google for calendar, Actual for finance); Household OS only displays. No write-back.
* **Owned:** Household OS is source of truth; the corresponding Google product is *retired for that job*. The panel/app can edit and check off.

| Surface | Bucket | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **Calendar** | Mirror (read-only) | Google Calendar stays source of truth; family keeps adding events on their phones; panel displays only. |
| **Grocery / shopping** | Owned | The §2.2 kanban board. Keep retired *for groceries*. |
| **Tasks / chores** | Owned | Household OS is source of truth for shared household to-dos; the e-ink panel checks them off (§6.2). Google Tasks retired for household use; family adds chores via the app/panel. |
| **Keep (general notes)** | Out of scope | Not integrated in v1. Keep stays as-is for ad-hoc family notes. |
| **Finance** | Mirror (read-only) | Actual is source of truth and the edit surface, *wrapped* at a subdomain (§2.4). Dashboard mirrors summaries + overspend alerts; edits are a hand-off into Actual, not a sync. |

This keeps each integration a one-directional data flow — a read for mirrors, a write-through for owned surfaces — with no reconciliation logic.

